# Target C — mixed-wall repacking for a convex exterior cap

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only research candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

Audited-working Section 165 protects either Type-I inactive container by placing every
replacement point in the full one-unit inner parallel body `C_{-1}`. For a canonical
exterior cap this erodes more boundary than the separation proof actually needs.

Canonical Sections 67, 80 and 84 describe each convex exterior pocket/cap by

- one artificial force-hull closing chord `[u,v]`; and
- a complementary polygonal path `W` made of actual unit contact edges between source
  vertices.

Only the artificial chord needs geometric one-unit erosion. Along the contact path,
unit-edge crossing geometry shows that it is enough to remain one unit from the
**boundary source vertices**. This note proves the resulting mixed-wall shield and the
same-cardinality strict-diameter repacking theorem, then gives a lattice-averaging
capacity criterion.

---

## 1. Canonical exterior-cap geometry

Let `A` be the triangle-free global diameter minimizer, `K=conv(A)`, and let `H` be
the complete actual diameter graph.

Let `C` be one canonical convex exterior cap selected in the Type-I alternative of
Sections 80/162. Its boundary is

\[
 \partial C=[u,v]\cup W,
\tag{1.1}
\]

where `[u,v]` is the force-hull closing chord and

\[
 W=(u=p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_\ell=v)
\tag{1.2}
\]

is the complementary old contact path with

\[
 |p_{i+1}-p_i|=1.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Let

\[
 Z_C=A\cap\operatorname{int}C,
 \qquad z_C=|Z_C|.
\]

Audited-working Section 162 supplies that the points of `Z_C` are contact-isolated and
that every source point not assigned to `Z_C` lies outside `int C`. The cap has
quadratic area/inactive population, linear perimeter/slack, and linear inradius in the
Type-I branch.

A source point may lie on the closing chord. Such a point is outside `int C`; the
mixed-wall guard below protects it directly through distance from the chord.

---

## 2. Mixed-wall safe region

Define

\[
 S_{mix}(C):=\left\{
 y\in\operatorname{int}C:
 \operatorname{dist}(y,[u,v])\ge1,
 \quad |y-p_i|\ge1\ \text{for every }0\le i\le\ell
 \right\}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

The full inner body is contained in this region:

\[
 \boxed{C_{-1}\subseteq S_{mix}(C).}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Indeed distance at least one from the whole boundary implies distance at least one
from the closing chord and from every contact-path vertex.

Define the recovered wall area

\[
 G_{wall}(C)
 :=\operatorname{area}(S_{mix}(C))-\operatorname{area}(C_{-1})\ge0.
\tag{2.3}
\]

No positive lower bound on `G_wall` is asserted here. Establishing a linear lower
bound from the long unit-contact path would be a useful next theorem.

---

## 3. Mixed-wall shield

We use the elementary contact-edge crossing fact proved in the internal contact-wall
module: if a segment of length `<1` properly crosses a unit edge `[p,q]` and its
outside endpoint is at distance at least one from `p,q`, then its inside endpoint is
at distance `<1` from both `p,q`.

### Theorem 3.1 — exterior mixed-wall shield

If `y in S_mix(C)` and `x` is any old source point outside `int C`, then

\[
 \boxed{|x-y|\ge1.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

#### Proof

Suppose `|x-y|<1`. Follow the segment from `y` toward `x` and let `r` be its first
intersection with `partial C`.

**Closing-chord interior.** If `r` lies in the relative interior of `[u,v]`, then

\[
 \operatorname{dist}(y,[u,v])\le |y-r|<|y-x|<1,
\]

contradicting (2.1).

**Boundary vertex.** If `r=p_i`, then `p_i` is one of the guarded contact-path
vertices. If `x=p_i`, (2.1) directly gives `|x-y|>=1`. Otherwise source unit
separation gives `|x-p_i|>=1`, and since `p_i` lies strictly between `x,y`,

\[
 |x-y|=|x-p_i|+|p_i-y|\ge2,
\]

a contradiction.

**Contact-edge interior.** Otherwise `r` lies in the relative interior of one unit
edge `[p_i,p_{i+1}]` of `W`. If `x` is one of its endpoints, the boundary-vertex guard
already gives `|x-y|>=1`. Otherwise source unit separation gives

\[
 |x-p_i|,|x-p_{i+1}|\ge1.
\]

The segment crosses the boundary edge properly at `r`. The two-endpoint crossing
lemma therefore gives

\[
 |y-p_i|<1,
 \qquad |y-p_{i+1}|<1,
\]

contradicting (2.1).

These cases exhaust the boundary of `C`. `square`

### Corollary 3.2 — simultaneous replacement

Any pairwise unit-separated set `Y subset S_mix(C)` is automatically unit-separated
from every old source point outside `int C`.

Thus the full one-unit collar along `W` is unnecessary.

---

## 4. Mixed-wall cover repacking

Let

\[
 M_{mix}(C)
 :=\sup\{|Y|:Y\subset S_{mix}(C),\ |y-y'|\ge1\}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Let `U` be a vertex cover of the complete actual diameter graph `H`. If

\[
 M_{mix}(C)\ge z_C+|U|,
\tag{4.2}
\]

choose a unit-separated `Y subset S_mix(C)` of that cardinality and put

\[
 A':=(A\setminus(Z_C\cup U))\cup Y.
\tag{4.3}
\]

### Theorem 4.1 — mixed-wall diameter-cover repacking

Under (4.2), `A'` is a same-cardinality unit-separated source with

\[
 \boxed{\operatorname{diam}A'<D.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

#### Proof

Every point of `Z_C` lies in `int C subset int K`, hence is not an endpoint of an
actual diameter edge. Therefore `Z_C cap U` is empty and cardinality is preserved.

Old-old unit separation is unchanged. New-new separation holds by construction, and
new-old separation follows from Corollary 3.2 because every retained old source lies
outside `int C`.

Since `U` covers every actual diameter edge, every retained old-old pair has distance
strictly below `D`.

Every new point lies in `int C`, hence in `int K`. For `x in K` and `y in int K`,
`|x-y|<D`: the convex source hull lies in the closed radius-`D` disk centered at `x`,
and an interior point of `K` cannot lie on that disk's boundary circle. Thus every
new-old and new-new distance is also strictly below `D`.

The finite set `A'` therefore has diameter strictly below `D`, contradicting global
diameter minimality. `square`

Taking a minimum vertex cover gives the survivor condition

\[
 \boxed{M_{mix}(C)-z_C<\tau(H).}
\tag{4.5}
\]

Because `C_{-1} subset S_mix(C)`, this strictly weakens the geometric protection
hypothesis in Section 165 and can only increase the available repacking capacity.

---

## 5. Lattice-averaging lower bound

Let `Lambda` be a unit triangular lattice, whose fundamental area is

\[
 a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

For a bounded measurable region `S`, averaging the number of points of
`(Lambda+t) cap S` over one fundamental translation cell gives

\[
 {\operatorname{area}(S)\over a_0}.
\]

Hence some translate has at least the floor of that average. Applied to the mixed-wall
region,

\[
 \boxed{
 M_{mix}(C)
 \ge
 \left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}(S_{mix}(C))\over a_0}\right\rfloor.
 }
\tag{5.1}
\]

The lattice is unit-separated, and every selected lattice point lies in the safe
region, so Theorem 3.1 supplies separation from the retained source automatically.

Therefore an explicit sufficient condition for Type-I exterior-cap elimination is

\[
 \boxed{
 \left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}(S_{mix}(C))\over a_0}\right\rfloor
 \ge z_C+\tau(H).
 }
\tag{5.2}
\]

Writing

\[
 A_{-1}=\operatorname{area}(C_{-1}),
\]

(2.3) gives

\[
 \operatorname{area}(S_{mix}(C))=A_{-1}+G_{wall}(C).
\tag{5.3}
\]

Thus the exact geometric improvement over the Section-165 lattice route is the
recovered area `G_wall/a_0` in packing slots.

No reverse Oler step occurs: (5.1) is a constructive translate-averaging lower bound.

---

## 6. What remains

This result removes the contact-side collar but does not yet prove that (5.2) holds in
every macroscopic exterior cap.

The next quantitative target is now precise:

> prove a lower bound for `G_wall(C)` from the long unit-contact boundary path and the
> Type-I linear-inradius geometry, strong enough to bridge the Section-165 capacity
> deficit.

A local straight unit-contact edge recovers a universal crescent between the endpoint
unit disks and the full distance-one edge strip. However, neighboring boundary
vertices or another nearby portion of the convex boundary can cut into that crescent.
Any global linear lower bound must control those overlaps using convexity, total
turning and the Type-I inradius; it is not asserted without proof.

The theorem keeps the full source cardinality, never applies the Section-57 affine
kernel to isolates, and uses the complete actual diameter graph in the cover step.

